// Optimal Solution: 
// This is a math trick for string rotation problems. If you concatenate a string to itself (s + s) the resulting double length string will contain every possible rotation of the original string as a substring 
// Eg: if s = "abcde" then s + s = "abcdeabcde"
// Look at the substrings of length 5 inside "abcdeabcde"
// abcde (0 shifts)
// bcdea (1 shift)
// cdeab (2 shifts) -> this is our goal
// deabc (3 shifts)
// eabcd (4 shifts)
// So the entire problem boils down to checking if goal is a substring of s + s

class Solution {
public:
    bool rotateString(string s, string goal) {
        if (s.length() != goal.length()) {
            return false;
        }

        //Concatenate s with itself 
        string doubled_s = s + s;

        //Check if goal is a substring of doubled_s
        //find() returns npos if the substring is not found 
        return doubled_s.find(goal) != string::npos;
    }
};

TC: O(n) 
concatenating string takes O(n) time. find() func is highly optimized 

SC: O(n) 
we have to allocate memory to store the new concatenated string doubled_s. (it is 2n character long)